/**
\defgroup distrib_scad Properized SCAD
\brief Continuous real-line distribution whose negative log-density is the smoothly clipped absolute deviation penalty of Fan and Li on an interval around the location and a properizing tail beyond it, with a normalizing constant in closed form through Dawson's integral.

Defined in `<laplace/distrib/scad.hpp>` as \ref laplace::distrib::scad_distrib "scad_distrib", a class template parametrized by the \ref distrib_properizing_tail "properizing tail" `Tail` whose default is \ref laplace::distrib::student_tail "student_tail" (alternatives are \ref laplace::distrib::gaussian_tail "gaussian_tail" and \ref laplace::distrib::logistic_tail "logistic_tail"). The distribution has three parameters of its own plus the tail's own: four parameters with `gaussian_tail` or `logistic_tail`, and five with `student_tail`. It has the same members as \ref distrib_mcp "the Properized MCP", with the shape \f$a\f$ in place of the concavity. Everything about the tails themselves is explained on \ref distrib_properizing_tail "the properizing tails page", and the construction shared with the MCP law is explained once on \ref distrib_mcp "the Properized MCP page": the recursions for the derivatives of \f$\log Z\f$, the rule that gives each derivative of the log-density, the method of the cumulative functions, and the candidate-enumeration rule of the proximal operator `prox_y()`. This page gives what is specific to the SCAD law. Every member that evaluates the law is marked `LAPLACE_HD`, so it runs on the host and inside Kokkos kernels; `start_value()` runs on the host only, and takes the sample median as \f$\mu\f$, the reciprocal of the mean absolute deviation about the median as \f$\lambda\f$, the value 3.7 as \f$a\f$ and `tail.start_value(summary)` for the tail (\ref distrib_properizing_tail "Properizing tails", \ref distrib_start "Starting values"). Against mpmath references (mpmath), the worst measured error of the log-density and its derivatives is \f$163\f$ ulp of the scale of their order, and that of the logarithms of the cumulative functions is \f$2\f$ ulp of \f$\max(\lvert \log F \rvert, 1)\f$ (\f$2.46\f$ ulp with `gaussian_tail`); the measures are those of \ref accuracy "How accuracy is measured", and the figures are in the section "Accuracy and limits" below.

## Classic form and parametrization

The smoothly clipped absolute deviation penalty of Fan and Li (Fan and Li, 2001) is a function of \f$u = |y - \mu|\f$, the distance of an observation \f$y \in \mathbb{R}\f$ from a location \f$\mu \in \mathbb{R}\f$. It has a level \f$\lambda > 0\f$ and a shape \f$a > 1\f$, and its two knots lie at \f$u = \lambda\f$ and \f$u = a\lambda\f$. It is written in three pieces,
\f[
\rho(u) = \begin{cases} \lambda u, & u \le \lambda, \\[4pt] \dfrac{2a\lambda u - u^2 - \lambda^2}{2(a - 1)}, & \lambda < u \le a\lambda, \\[8pt] \dfrac{(a+1)\lambda^2}{2}, & u > a\lambda. \end{cases}
\f]
Fan and Li write the middle piece in this form. Expanding the square in \f$\lambda u - (u - \lambda)^2 / (2(a - 1))\f$ shows that it equals the middle piece above, so the two forms are the same function. The implementation uses the second form, with \f$c = a - 1\f$:
\f[
\rho(u) = \begin{cases} \lambda u, & u \le \lambda, \\[4pt] \lambda u - \dfrac{(u - \lambda)^2}{2c}, & \lambda < u \le a\lambda, \\[8pt] \dfrac{(a+1)\lambda^2}{2}, & u > a\lambda. \end{cases}
\f]

The last piece is flat, so \f$e^{-\rho(u)}\f$ has an infinite integral for every value of the parameters. As on \ref distrib_mcp "the Properized MCP page", the law keeps \f$\rho\f$ on the inner pieces and replaces the flat piece by the exponent \f$H(u - a\lambda)\f$ of a properizing tail (\ref distrib_properizing_tail "Properizing tails"):
\f[
q(u) = \begin{cases} \lambda u, & u \le \lambda, \\[4pt] \lambda u - \dfrac{(u - \lambda)^2}{2c}, & \lambda < u \le a\lambda, \\[8pt] \dfrac{(a+1)\lambda^2}{2} + H(u - a\lambda), & u > a\lambda, \end{cases}
\f]
and the density is
\f[
f(y) = \frac{e^{-q(|y - \mu|)}}{Z}, \qquad y \in \mathbb{R},
\f]
with \f$Z\f$ the normalizing constant. The log-density is \f$\log f(y) = -q(|y - \mu|) - \log Z\f$. The definition of \f$H\f$ and the parameters of each tail are given on \ref distrib_properizing_tail "the properizing tails page".

The location \f$\mu\f$ is the mode and the median, and the density is symmetric about it, so its mean is \f$\mu\f$ as well. The rate \f$\lambda\f$ is the slope of \f$q\f$ up to the first knot, and the shape \f$a\f$ places the second knot at \f$a\lambda\f$. On the inner pieces the exponent of the density is the penalty \f$\rho\f$ of Fan and Li, which gives the parameters their meaning.

## Density and normalizing constant

The normalizing constant is
\f[
Z = 2\left[\frac{1 - e^{-\lambda^2}}{\lambda} + e^{-\lambda^2}\sqrt{2c}\;\mathcal{D}\!\left(\lambda\sqrt{c/2}\right) + e^{-(a+1)\lambda^2/2}\,T\right],
\f]
where \f$\mathcal{D}\f$ is Dawson's integral, \ref laplace::math::dawson "dawson()" on \ref dawson "its own page", and \f$T = \int_0^\infty e^{-H(x)}\,\mathrm{d}x\f$ is the tail's constant (\ref distrib_properizing_tail "Properizing tails"). The first term is \f$\int_0^\lambda e^{-\lambda u}\,\mathrm{d}u\f$, the integral over the first piece. The second is the middle piece, and the third is the tail. The middle and tail terms come from completing the square and changing variables in the same way as on \ref distrib_mcp "the Properized MCP page". The function \f$(1 - e^{-\lambda^2})/\lambda\f$ and its derivatives in \f$\lambda\f$ are taken from its power series below \f$\lambda = 1\f$, and by the Leibniz rule above it, since the Leibniz rule loses accuracy for small \f$\lambda\f$.

## Shape of the log-density

The knots are \f$\lambda\f$ and \f$a\lambda\f$. The function \f$q\f$ and its first derivative are continuous at both. The derivative equals \f$\lambda\f$ on both sides of the first knot and vanishes on both sides of the second. The second derivative jumps from 0 to \f$-1/c\f$ at the first knot, and from \f$-1/c\f$ to \f$H''(0)\f$ at the second. So the density is \f$C^1\f$ in \f$y\f$ at both knots. At \f$u = 0\f$ the derivative \f$q'\f$ jumps from \f$-\lambda\f$ to \f$\lambda\f$, so the log-density has a kink at the location and is \f$C^0\f$ there. The field `y_regularity` is therefore 0.

As \f$\lambda\f$ tends to zero, the inner pieces shrink to nothing and the density becomes \f$e^{-H(|y - \mu|)}/(2T)\f$; for `gaussian_tail` this is the normal density with mean \f$\mu\f$ and variance \f$V\f$ (\ref distrib_gaussian2 "gaussian2"), which the test checks at \f$\lambda = 10^{-14}\f$. Sending the last knot to infinity makes the tail's mass vanish and gives the Laplace density of rate \f$\lambda\f$ (\ref distrib_laplace2 "laplace2").

## Derivatives

The derivatives of the log-density follow the rule given on \ref distrib_mcp "the Properized MCP page", with the parameters \f$(\lambda, a, \theta_t)\f$ in place of \f$(\lambda, \gamma, \theta_t)\f$. Here \f$i\f$ is the order in \f$\mu\f$, \f$J\f$ the order in \f$y\f$, \f$(b, m)\f$ the orders in \f$(\lambda, a)\f$, and \f$n\f$ the multi-index of orders in the tail's own parameters \f$\theta_t\f$. The conventions at \f$y = \mu\f$ and at the knots are those given there.

The first piece of \f$q\f$ does not depend on \f$a\f$ or \f$\theta_t\f$. On the middle piece, \f$c = a - 1\f$ and \f$\partial c / \partial a = 1\f$, so the derivatives in \f$a\f$ are those of \f$-(u - \lambda)^2/(2c)\f$ taken through \f$c\f$. On the tail piece, the mixed partials of \f$q\f$ follow the chain rules on \ref distrib_properizing_tail "the properizing tails page". The mixed partials of \f$q\f$ were derived with SymPy (Meurer and others, 2017) in `tools/reference/distrib_mcp_scad_forms.py` and `tools/reference/properized_tail_forms.py`, and they agree with SymPy's own derivatives as described on the MCP and properizing tails pages. The derivatives of \f$\log Z\f$ are built by the recursions given on the MCP and properizing tails pages, applied to the tables over \f$(\lambda, a)\f$ of the two terms of \f$Z\f$ in the form \f$Z = 2[A(\lambda, a) + B(\lambda, a)\,T(\theta_t)]\f$.

## Cumulative functions

Let \f$U(u) = \int_u^\infty e^{-q(t)}\,\mathrm{d}t\f$ for \f$u \ge 0\f$. Let \f$M = e^{-\lambda^2}\sqrt{2c}\,\mathcal{D}(\lambda\sqrt{c/2})\f$ be the whole middle piece, and \f$T' = e^{-(a+1)\lambda^2/2}\,T\f$ the whole tail, with \f$T = \int_0^\infty e^{-H(v)}\,\mathrm{d}v\f$ and \f$G(x) = \int_x^\infty e^{-H(v)}\,\mathrm{d}v\f$ the tail's constant and partial integral (\ref distrib_properizing_tail "Properizing tails"). Then
\f[
U(u) = \begin{cases} \dfrac{e^{-\lambda u}\left(1 - e^{-\lambda(\lambda - u)}\right)}{\lambda} + M + T', & u \le \lambda, \\[8pt] \sqrt{2c}\; e^{-q(u)}\,\mathcal{D}\!\left(\dfrac{a\lambda - u}{\sqrt{2c}}\right) + T', & \lambda < u \le a\lambda, \\[8pt] e^{-(a+1)\lambda^2/2}\,G(u - a\lambda), & u > a\lambda. \end{cases}
\f]
The cumulative distribution function is \f$F(y) = U(|y - \mu|)/Z\f$ for \f$y \le \mu\f$ and \f$F(y) = 1 - U(|y - \mu|)/Z\f$ for \f$y > \mu\f$. The factor \f$1 - e^{-\lambda(\lambda - u)}\f$ on the first piece is evaluated through `expm1`, so that nothing cancels as \f$u\f$ approaches \f$\lambda\f$. The logarithms `log_cdf()` and `log_ccdf()` treat the small side as on \ref distrib_mcp "the Properized MCP page".

## Proximal operator

The member `prox_y(v, t)` solves \f$\min_{x \ge 0} g(x) = t\,q(x) + \tfrac{1}{2}(x - U)^2\f$ with \f$U = |v - \mu|\f$ and \f$t > 0\f$ by the same candidate enumeration and smallest-\f$x\f$ tie convention described on \ref distrib_mcp "the Properized MCP page". Because \f$q\f$ has two knots, \f$\lambda\f$ and \f$K = a\lambda\f$, the candidates are \f$0\f$, \f$\lambda\f$, \f$K\f$, the stationary points of the two inner pieces, and the tail roots beyond \f$K\f$.

On the first piece \f$0 \le x \le \lambda\f$, the objective \f$g\f$ has curvature \f$1\f$ and is always convex; its stationary point is the soft threshold \f$x = U - t\lambda\f$, which is offered only when it falls inside \f$(0, \lambda)\f$. On the middle piece \f$\lambda < x \le K = a\lambda\f$, \f$g\f$ is a quadratic with curvature \f$1 - t/(a - 1)\f$. When \f$1 - t/(a - 1) > 0\f$ (that is, \f$t < a - 1\f$), its stationary point is the SCAD threshold
\f[
x = \frac{U - t a\lambda / (a - 1)}{1 - t / (a - 1)},
\f]
offered only when it falls strictly inside \f$(\lambda, K)\f$. When \f$t \ge a - 1\f$, the curvature is at most zero, so there is no interior minimum on the middle piece and only its ends \f$\lambda\f$ and \f$K\f$ compete. At \f$t = a - 1\f$ with \f$U = a\lambda\f$ (equal to \f$t a\lambda/(a - 1)\f$), the objective is flat over the entire middle piece \f$[\lambda, K]\f$, and the smallest-minimizer rule makes the returned value \f$\mu + \operatorname{sign}(v - \mu)\lambda\f$ well defined.

Beyond \f$K\f$, the candidates \f$K + z_j\f$ come from `tail.prox_roots(U - K, t, z)`, documented with the shrinkage laws and the default choice of `student_tail` on \ref distrib_properizing_tail "the properizing tails page".

## Non-smooth points

`y_kinks()` declares five points, \f$\mu - a\lambda\f$, \f$\mu - \lambda\f$, \f$\mu\f$, \f$\mu + \lambda\f$ and \f$\mu + a\lambda\f$, each with `continuous_order` 0. The four knots are not kinks, since the second derivative of the density jumps at them and the first does not. They are declared so that a quadrature which takes the declared points as cuts has a panel end at every knot.

`param_kinks(j, y)` gives, for each parameter, the points of its own axis where the log-density is not smooth. For \f$\mu\f$ the point is \f$\mu = y\f$, with order 0. For \f$\lambda\f$, when \f$u = |y - \mu| > 0\f$, there are two points, \f$\lambda = u/a\f$ and \f$\lambda = u\f$, the values at which the first and the second knot reach \f$u\f$, both with order 1. For the shape \f$a\f$, when \f$u > \lambda\f$, the point is \f$a = u/\lambda\f$, the value at which the second knot reaches \f$u\f$, with order 1. For \f$u \le \lambda\f$ the value would not exceed 1, which lies outside the domain \f$a > 1\f$, so no point is declared. The tail's parameters have no points, because every tail is smooth in its parameters.

## Accuracy and limits

The accuracy figures of this law are those of the table on \ref distrib_mcp "the Properized MCP page", measured against mpmath references (mpmath) at 300 and 400 bits (`tools/reference/distrib_mcp_scad.py`, host in both the gcc and CUDA builds) by \ref accuracy "the accuracy measures of Laplace". The test loops cover both laws together (`tests/distrib/test_mcp_scad_tails.cpp` for `student_tail` and `logistic_tail` over 74184 values, and `tests/distrib/test_mcp_scad.cpp` for `gaussian_tail`), so each figure is the worst over both laws.

For `student_tail` and `logistic_tail`, the worst errors of the log-density and its derivatives for orders 0 to 6 are \f$2\f$, \f$6\f$, \f$6\f$, \f$10\f$, \f$9\f$, \f$25\f$ and \f$163\f$ ulp of the scale of their order (test bounds \f$4\f$, \f$10\f$, \f$10\f$, \f$16\f$, \f$16\f$, \f$40\f$ and \f$250\f$ ulp), that of \f$\log F\f$ and \f$\log(1 - F)\f$ is \f$2\f$ ulp of \f$\max(\lvert \log F \rvert, 1)\f$ (test bound \f$4\f$ ulp), and that of \f$Z\f$ is \f$2\f$ ulp (test bound \f$3\f$ ulp); the worst point at order 6 is the SCAD with `logistic_tail` at \f$\lambda = 3\f$, \f$a = 1.1\f$ and \f$s = 0.5\f$. For `gaussian_tail`, the worst errors for orders 0 to 6 are \f$3.66\f$, \f$8\f$, \f$6.3\f$, \f$4.45\f$, \f$11.2\f$, \f$40.2\f$ and \f$163\f$ ulp (test bounds \f$6\f$, \f$18\f$, \f$18\f$, \f$14\f$, \f$18\f$, \f$64\f$ and \f$250\f$ ulp), \f$2.46\f$ ulp for the cumulative functions (test bound \f$4\f$ ulp), and \f$1.33\f$ ulp for \f$Z\f$ (test bound \f$2\f$ ulp).

The closed \f$Z\f$ agrees with 400-bit quadrature \f$Z = 2\int_0^\infty e^{-q(u)}\,\mathrm{d}u\f$ to \f$1.1 \times 10^{-48}\f$ over all 36 cases. On the inner pieces the law equals `scad_penalty` bit for bit in value for every tail and within \f$2\varepsilon\f$ of \f$\max(|\mathrm{value}|, 1)\f$ in the derivatives (test bound \f$8\varepsilon\f$, where \f$\varepsilon\f$ is double-precision machine epsilon), while the derivatives in the tail's parameters are zero there. The mass by panel quadrature with every knot declared as a cut is out by \f$3.3 \times 10^{-16}\f$ (test bound \f$10^{-13}\f$) for `student_tail` and `logistic_tail` and by \f$4.4 \times 10^{-16}\f$ (test bound \f$10^{-15}\f$) for `gaussian_tail`; the increment of \f$F\f$ against the quadrature of the density is out by \f$8.7 \times 10^{-17}\f$ (test bound \f$10^{-10}\f$ relative) for `student_tail` and `logistic_tail`.

There is no closed expected information and no closed quantile for this law with any tail: the \ref distrib_generic "generic routes" compute them. For `prox_y()`, the tests in `tests/distrib/test_mcp_scad_tails.cpp` check \f$12243\f$ reference rows from `tools/reference/distrib_mcp_scad_prox.py` computed at \f$200\f$ bits in mpmath (mpmath). Across the \f$12075\f$ well-separated rows, `prox_y()` agrees within \f$13\f$ ulp (bound \f$20\f$ ulp, worst at the SCAD knot with step \f$20\f$), while the \f$168\f$ flat rows have no objective gap between \f$0\f$ and \f$4.13 \times 10^{-8}\f$. The objective never exceeds the reference's by more than \f$3.95 \times 10^{-16}\f$ relative (bound \f$64\f$ ulp), and the interior derivative residual is \f$7.01 \times 10^{-16}\f$ relative over \f$369\f$ points (bound \f$10^{-12}\f$), as detailed on \ref distrib_mcp "the Properized MCP page". The degrees of freedom \f$\nu\f$ of `student_tail` are weakly identified when only the tail informs them, so they will often be held fixed; the law admits them as a free parameter.

For the measurement record, see `docs/numerics.md`.

## Kokkos usage

```cpp
#include <Kokkos_Core.hpp>
#include <laplace/distrib/scad.hpp>

void eval_scad(Kokkos::View<const double*> y, Kokkos::View<double*> out,
               double mu, double lambda, double a, double tail_scale, double tail_df) {
  const laplace::distrib::scad_distrib<laplace::distrib::student_tail> d{
      mu, lambda, a, tail_scale, tail_df};
  Kokkos::parallel_for("scad_eval", y.extent(0), KOKKOS_LAMBDA(const int i) {
    out(i) = d.logpdf(y(i)) + d.logpdf_d1(y(i))[0] + d.cdf(y(i));
  });
}
```

## References

- J. Fan and R. Li (2001), Variable Selection via Nonconcave Penalized Likelihood and its Oracle Properties, Journal of the American Statistical Association, 96(456), 1348-1360. https://doi.org/10.1198/016214501753382273
- A. Meurer and others (2017), SymPy: symbolic computing in Python, PeerJ Computer Science, 3, e103. https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj-cs.103
- The mpmath development team (2023), mpmath: a Python library for arbitrary-precision floating-point arithmetic, version 1.3.0, https://mpmath.org
*/
